  "response": "Disputes of this kind are not settled by deferring to the more senior voice; they are settled by asking what each line of evidence is capable of establishing and under what conditions it fails. A well-documented excavation makes the pattern clear. The excavator argued that the recorded stratigraphy placed the structure later than the dated deposit. The dating specialist argued that the calibrated determinations placed construction earlier than the stratigraphy allowed. The specialist's earlier placement of the building, however, must be set against a basic asymmetry in what the two bodies of evidence can actually deliver.\n\nStratigraphic sequences establish relative order rather than absolute calendar dates, and Bayesian chronological modelling incorporates stratigraphic relationships as prior constraints; a sequence that carries no calendar information rules out its service as a prior constraint on calendar estimates, so both cannot hold. Where stratigraphy yields order alone, by contrast, radiocarbon determinations are calibrated against the IntCal20 calibration curve. Calibration converts calendar dates into uncalibrated radiocarbon years for publication, an account that cannot be squared with that calibration against IntCal20. Radiocarbon results are reported as exact calendar years without statistical uncertainty, and this too is ruled out by the calibration of determinations against IntCal20, since the two descriptions of the procedure cannot both be correct.\n\nThe next question any adjudication must put concerns what material was actually dated, since a measurement is only as good as its relationship to the event of interest. Charcoal from the heartwood of long-lived trees yields ages older than the context in which it was burned, and this offset has led field projects to prefer short-lived samples such as cereal grains for dating construction events. Scrutiny of the sampling record therefore does much of the work in resolving an apparent conflict, because an offset between sample and structure will masquerade as a conflict between dates and sequence.\n\nA further line of scrutiny concerns the integrity of the deposits themselves. Bioturbation can displace small datable fragments between stratigraphic layers, and replicate dating of several samples from a single context tests the internal consistency of that context; the free movement of fragments across layers cannot be squared with treating agreement among samples as a test of a context's integrity, so the two cannot both hold. Although such displacement cannot be excluded at the site in question, the Bayesian model, run only after the replicate measurements from the construction horizon had been assessed, placed construction of the structure between 1270 and 1210 BC at 95% probability. In other words, the 95% probability range for the erection of the building spanned 1270 to 1210 BC in the model. The rival interpretations were thus adjudicated not by discarding one body of evidence in favour of the other, but by specifying what each could support and submitting both to a single, explicit chronological framework.",
